---
import ResearchFigure from "../../components/ResearchFigure.astro";
import type { CasePart } from "../types";
import overview from "./agent-figure-01-original.png";
import method from "./agent-figure-02-original.png";
import ranking from "./agent-figure-03-original.png";
import statements from "./agent-figure-04-original.png";
import counterparts from "./agent-figure-05-original.png";

interface Props {
  part: CasePart;
}
const { part } = Astro.props;
const article = "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36813";
const models = [
  "Qwen3.5-9B",
  "Qwen3.8-27B",
  "Gemma-4-12B",
  "Gemma-4-31B",
  "Seed-OSS-36B",
  "GLM-4-32B",
  "Ministral-3-14B",
  "Olmo-3.1-32B",
];
---

{part === "intro" && (
  <p>
    I contributed to the design and psychological grounding of user profiles,
    considering how past behavior, personality and interaction partners inform
    the interpretation of what people say.
  </p>
)}

{part === "visual" && (
  <ResearchFigure
    src={overview}
    eager
    class="agent-original-figure"
    alt="Complete original Figure 1. A shows a user playing with five Big Five agent personas; earlier decisions and current dialogue are passed to separate LLM predictors, whose forecasts are compared with the recorded decision. B shows nearly unchanged overall accuracy but gains for kept statements and losses for broken statements, alongside larger persona differences in human than predicted cooperation."
  >
    <a href={article}>Figure 1, Zhang et al., 2026.</a> Five GPT-4o agents
    interacted with people. Eight separate LLMs later predicted the recorded
    decisions. The “All” accuracy group comprises rounds with a stated decision.
    <span class="agent-source-note">
      Original figures: Zhang et al., arXiv:2610.36813v1 (2026), CC BY-NC 4.0.
    </span>
  </ResearchFigure>
)}

{part === "body" && (
  <>
    <nav class="agent-result-nav" aria-label="Sections on this page">
      <a href="#agent-method">Method</a>
      <a href="#agent-ranking">Ranking decisions</a>
      <a href="#agent-statements">Words and deeds</a>
      <a href="#agent-counterparts">Counterparts</a>
      <a href="#agent-record">Improving forecasts</a>
    </nav>

    <section class="portfolio-section" aria-labelledby="agent-method">
      <h2 id="agent-method">From recorded games to offline forecasts</h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        The study reused Personality-1260: 42 participants played six rounds
        with each of five agents prompted to express different Big Five traits.
        They could talk before choosing to cooperate or defect. Both players’
        choices determined the rewards.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        The predictors received information recorded before each decision:
        earlier actions by both players, dialogue, or combinations of the two.
        Nineteen information conditions tested what each source added.
      </p>
      <table class="agent-table agent-scope-table" role="table">
        <caption>Two analysis scopes</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col">Analysis</th>
            <th scope="col">Games</th>
            <th scope="col">Rounds</th>
            <th scope="col">Users</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row">Human behavior across agents</th>
            <td data-label="Games">1–5</td>
            <td data-label="Rounds">1,260</td>
            <td data-label="Users">42</td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row">Predictions and matched behavior</th>
            <td data-label="Games">2–5</td>
            <td data-label="Rounds">1,008</td>
            <td data-label="Users">42</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={method}
        class="agent-original-figure"
        alt="Complete original Figure 2. Decision records R come from the current game or earlier games. Dialogue D comes from the current round, current game or earlier games. A four-by-five matrix contains 19 tested combinations, excluding the empty condition. Eight predictors output cooperation probabilities, evaluated against words and deeds, agent persona and trait compatibility. A separate manipulation inserts a stated decision into the prompt."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 2.</a> R denotes earlier decisions; D denotes
        dialogue. Prediction analyses start at game 2 so that an earlier game is
        available. Dialogue and the later action were recorded separately.
      </ResearchFigure>
    </section>

    <section class="portfolio-section" aria-labelledby="agent-ranking">
      <h2 id="agent-ranking">History ranked decisions well</h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Earlier decisions reached AUC .80, compared with .70 for dialogue alone
        and .79 for both. Adding decision records improved ranking across all 12
        tested comparisons; adding dialogue to an available record brought no
        detectable gain. AUC measures whether cooperation ranks above defection,
        rather than the percentage of correct answers.
      </p>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={ranking}
        class="agent-original-figure"
        alt="Complete original Figure 3, panels a–d. AUC is grouped by decision-record information and dialogue information, for all prediction rounds and for rounds with a stated decision. All condition legends, 95 percent user-bootstrap intervals, chance and record-only reference lines, and Holm-corrected comparison brackets are preserved."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 3.</a> All results use games 2–5. The lower
        panels restrict the analysis to rounds with a stated decision. Error
        bars are 95% user-bootstrap intervals; brackets show Holm-corrected
        comparisons.
      </ResearchFigure>
    </section>

    <section class="portfolio-section" aria-labelledby="agent-statements">
      <h2 id="agent-statements">Similar accuracy concealed different errors</h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Adding the current dialogue barely changed accuracy on stated-decision
        rounds, from .73 to .74. It raised accuracy from .76 to .95 on 470 kept
        statements, but lowered it from .65 to .17 on 176 broken statements.
        Both changes were significant after correction.
      </p>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={statements}
        class="agent-original-figure"
        alt="Complete original Figure 4, panels a–d. Open and filled points show predictions before and after adding text; black bars show observed rates. A separates kept and broken statements. B separates stated actions chosen earlier from those not yet chosen. C tests earlier dialogue with or without a prior broken statement. D inserts a sentence matching or contradicting the recorded decision. All intervals, axes, group labels and significance brackets remain."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 4.</a> The four panels compare accuracy,
        predicted word-keeping, predicted cooperation and agreement with the
        recorded decision. In panel d, the sentence changes the predictor’s
        input; the recorded human decision stays fixed.
      </ResearchFigure>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        When a statement named an action the user had not yet chosen in that
        game, predicted word-keeping rose from .13 to .69; the observed rate was
        .40 (53 rounds from 20 users). Earlier broken statements were only
        partly used: adding earlier dialogue lowered predicted cooperation from
        .60 to .49, still above the observed .29.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        One inserted sentence moved agreement with the recorded decision from
        .75 to .98 when it matched that decision, and to .08 when it
        contradicted it. This tests how strongly the forecast responds to a
        statement, without showing that the statement changes human behavior.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="portfolio-section" aria-labelledby="agent-counterparts">
      <h2 id="agent-counterparts">People varied more with their counterpart</h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Across all five games, people cooperated more with the Agreeableness
        than Extraversion agent (72.2% versus 56.0%) and kept more statements
        (82.4% versus 68.1%). Other persona pairs did not differ reliably after
        correction. How often people stated a decision also showed no detectable
        persona difference.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        On the matched prediction rounds, the same agents elicited 70.6% versus
        54.5% cooperation, but predicted cooperation was 64.5% versus 63.1%. AUC
        did not differ reliably by persona. The near-zero pooled cooperation
        bias concealed a 14.6-point bias difference between these two agents.
      </p>
      <ResearchFigure
        src={counterparts}
        class="agent-original-figure"
        alt="Complete original Figure 5, panels a–i, with all five persona colors and icons. A and B show human cooperation and word-keeping distributions. C shows AUC, D and E prediction bias by persona. F and G show relative cooperation and word-keeping against trait compatibility; H and I show their prediction biases. Cooperation rises with compatibility while cooperation bias becomes more negative; the two word-keeping slopes are nonsignificant."
      >
        <a href={article}>Figure 5.</a> Panels a, b, f and g use all five games;
        c–e, h and i use games 2–5. Bias is predicted minus observed
        probability, in percentage points. Relative rates subtract each user’s
        own average across agents.
      </ResearchFigure>
      <h3>Compatibility mattered for cooperation</h3>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Compatibility means the user’s score on the trait expressed by the
        agent. Relative cooperation rose by 3.0 percentage points per standard
        deviation of compatibility, while prediction bias fell by 3.3 points.
        Neither word-keeping nor its prediction bias showed a detectable
        compatibility relationship. These trait analyses were exploratory and
        uncorrected.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="portfolio-section" aria-labelledby="agent-record">
      <h2 id="agent-record">Link statements to what happened next</h2>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        A supplementary evaluation added an explicit record of what the user had
        said and then chosen in earlier rounds. This reduced the two
        overestimates, although cooperation after an earlier broken statement
        remained above the observed rate.
      </p>
      <table class="agent-table" role="table">
        <caption>
          Adding the earlier said/did record to current-game decisions and
          current dialogue
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col">Prediction</th>
            <th scope="col">Before record</th>
            <th scope="col">With record</th>
            <th scope="col">Observed</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row">Keeping a statement of an action not yet chosen</th>
            <td data-label="Before record">.69</td>
            <td data-label="With record">.40</td>
            <td data-label="Observed">.40</td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row">Cooperating after an earlier broken statement</th>
            <td data-label="Before record">.60</td>
            <td data-label="With record">.40</td>
            <td data-label="Observed">.29</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="portfolio-copy">
        Providing personality scores also helped: the compatibility-related bias
        slope changed from −3.3 to −2.5 points per standard deviation. Its
        remaining interval included zero. Both the full Big Five scores and the
        single compatibility score produced similar improvements.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-note">
        The study used short, low-stakes games with Chinese university students.
        Persona prompts changed both agents’ language and actions, so their
        effects cannot be isolated here. The results concern these offline
        forecasts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <details class="portfolio-methods agent-details">
      <summary>Models, analysis subsets and statistical details</summary>
      <h3>Game, predictors and inference</h3>
      <p>
        Mutual cooperation earned two points each. Unilateral defection earned
        three for the defector and zero for the cooperator; mutual defection
        earned zero. The agent’s decision was revealed only after the user’s.
      </p>
      <p>
        The eight instruction-tuned predictors were {models.join(", ")}. They
        ran locally with Chinese prompts, zero-shot and with reasoning disabled.
        Cooperation-versus-defection log-odds were averaged across both
        answer-letter orders before conversion to probabilities. Results
        summarize the eight predictors. The five interaction agents all used
        GPT-4o.
      </p>
      <p>
        Uncertainty used 50,000 user-bootstrap draws, retaining each sampled
        user’s games and rounds together. Displayed comparison families used
        Holm correction. A model difference counted as significant only with the
        same sign under both letter orders. Compatibility slopes pooled agents
        and were not multiplicity-corrected.
      </p>
      <h3>Statement labels and analysis subsets</h3>
      <p>
        Two researchers labeled dialogue without seeing the later decision.
        Agreement was κ = .97 for cooperation, defection and no stated decision.
        Prediction rounds included 646 stated decisions from 41 users: 470 kept
        and 176 broken. Analyses requiring an earlier round excluded round 1.
        The not-yet-chosen group contained 53 rounds from 20 users; the
        sentence-insertion comparison used 308 rounds from 40 users with no
        stated decision.
      </p>
      <h3>Ranking and statement effects</h3>
      <p>
        AUC was .80 [.76, .85] for all decision records, .70 [.64, .76] for all
        dialogue, and .79 [.75, .83] for both. Adding records raised AUC in 12
        of 12 all-round comparisons and 11 of 12 stated-decision comparisons.
        Across nonempty record levels, dialogue conditions differed in 0 of 18
        comparisons in each subset.
      </p>
      <p>
        Current dialogue changed accuracy by +.20 [.15, .25] for kept statements
        and −.48 [−.52, −.44] for broken ones (both corrected p &lt; .001). For
        previously chosen stated actions, predicted word-keeping rose from .65
        to .90 against observed .73. For an action not yet chosen, observed
        word-keeping was .40 [.25, .58]; the forecast moved from below to above
        that rate (both corrected p &lt; .001).
      </p>
      <p>
        Relative to swapping the user’s previous decision in the prompt, an
        inserted cooperation statement changed prediction log-odds by a mean
        ratio of 1.34 across models. The corresponding behavioral-regression
        coefficient ratio was .35 [.11, .68]; the difference was .99 [.65, 1.38]
        (corrected p &lt; .001). The first ratio measures model input
        sensitivity; the second measures association in recorded human behavior.
      </p>
      <h3>Persona and compatibility</h3>
      <p>
        All-game Agreeableness–Extraversion differences were +16.3 points [6.7,
        25.8] for cooperation (corrected p = .008) and +14.3 [4.7, 24.5] for
        word-keeping (p = .022). On prediction rounds, cooperation bias differed
        between Extraversion and Agreeableness (14.6 points, p = .013) and
        Extraversion and Neuroticism (13.9, p = .018). No persona’s individual
        bias differed from zero after correction. The Extraversion–Agreeableness
        word-keeping bias contrast was nonsignificant (p = .057); pooled
        cooperation bias was −0.1 [−4.6, 4.4] points.
      </p>
      <p>
        Relative cooperation increased by 3.0 points/SD [0.2, 6.0] (p = .036);
        relative word-keeping did not (+0.1, p = .91). Cooperation-bias slope
        was −3.3 [−6.4, −0.4] (p = .025), whereas word-keeping-bias slope was
        −1.2 (p = .42). On matched prediction rounds, observed cooperation rose
        by 4.1 points/SD and predicted cooperation by 0.8.
      </p>
      <h3>Supplementary prompt information</h3>
      <p>
        Adding Big Five scores changed the bias slope to −2.5 [−5.3, 0.3] (p =
        .082), an improvement of 0.9 [0.2, 1.5] (p = .005). Adding the
        compatibility score changed it to −2.5 [−5.6, 0.4] (p = .090), an
        improvement of 0.8 [0.4, 1.2] (p &lt; .001). The two additions did not
        differ (p = .83).
      </p>
      <p>
        For the two groups in the said/did table, adding earlier dialogue
        instead of the explicit record produced probabilities of .60 and .49.
        Both exceeded the record’s .40 (corrected p &lt; .001). In groups with a
        previously chosen stated action or no earlier broken statement, the
        record changed predictions by less than .05. These supplementary
        conditions are reported in Section 5.1 and Appendices C–D of the{" "}
        <a href={article}>preprint</a>.
      </p>
    </details>
  </>
)}

<style>
  .agent-result-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem 1.4rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  .agent-source-note {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }
  :global(.agent-original-figure) {
    width: 100%;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  h2 {
    scroll-margin-top: 6rem;
  }
  .portfolio-section > h3 {
    margin-top: 1.8rem;
  }
  .agent-table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .agent-table caption {
    text-align: left;
    color: var(--portfolio-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.65rem;
  }
  .agent-table :is(th, td) {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--portfolio-line);
    padding: 0.75rem 0.9rem;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .agent-table thead {
    background: var(--portfolio-surface);
  }
  .agent-table th {
    color: var(--portfolio-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .agent-table td {
    color: var(--portfolio-muted);
  }
  .agent-details h3 {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 47.99rem) {
    .agent-table thead {
      position: absolute;
      width: 1px;
      height: 1px;
      overflow: hidden;
      clip-path: inset(50%);
    }
    .agent-table :is(tbody, tr, th, td) {
      display: block;
    }
    .agent-table tr {
      border-top: 1px solid var(--portfolio-line);
      margin: 1.2rem 0;
    }
    .agent-table :is(th, td) {
      padding: 10px 12px;
    }
    .agent-table th {
      background: var(--portfolio-surface);
    }
    .agent-table td::before {
      content: attr(data-label) ": ";
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--portfolio-ink);
    }
  }
</style>
